-*   **Chunk Dimension:** Slices along the Z-axis are the primary example [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Overlap Size:** Not given in the sources. Must be chosen to cover the stencil size of the rendering/extraction algorithm (e.g., 1-2 voxels for Marching Cubes).
+*   **Slice Order:** Must match storage order for efficient seeking [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Naive non-overlapping division causes black stripes or discontinuities because opacity/color calculations are missing at the boundary where data division occurs [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Image Stretching:** If overlap slices are not removed before compositing, the final image will be stretched due to the addition of extra slices [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Load Imbalance:** For uniform-resolution data, this method is efficient. For adaptive-resolution data, workload may not be equal, requiring different partitioning strategies [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Naive non-overlapping division causes black stripes or missing geometry at boundaries because opacity/color calculations are missing at the division line [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Image Stretching:** If overlap slices are not removed before final compositing, the image will be stretched [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Uneven Workload:** For uniform resolution data, workload is proportional to slice count. If $S$ is not divisible by $P$, some processors get $\lfloor S/P \rfloor + 1$ slices and others $\lfloor S/P \rfloor$ [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Adaptive Resolution:** This method is designed for uniform resolution data. For adaptive-resolution data, workload balancing requires different strategies (e.g., dividing by computational cost rather than slice count) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Chunk-based World Streaming
-Streams chunks from disk to memory as needed, focusing on temporal/spatial traversal rather than static MPI partitioning [G].
+### Object-space decomposition with local clustering
+Partitions the scene geometry rather than the data grid. Better for sparse or complex geometry. [Link](4156-object-space-decomposition-with-local-clustering.md)
 
-### Chunk-based Training
-Applies chunking to machine learning training data for volume rendering, focusing on data augmentation and batch processing [G].
-
-### Chunk Trees
-Uses a hierarchical tree structure for adaptive chunking, allowing for variable resolution and more complex spatial queries [G].
-
-### Object-space decomposition with local clustering
-Partitions space based on object density rather than uniform grid, useful for scenes with varying object distribution [G].
-
-### Uniform Block Partitioning with Acceleration
-Uses uniform blocks but adds acceleration structures (like BVH) for ray traversal, differing from the simple slice-based approach [G].
-
-### Moving Walls Algorithm
-A dynamic partitioning technique for time-varying data, moving a "wall" of processed data through the volume [G].
-
-### Tide: Trajectory-Adaptive Differential Streaming
-Adapts streaming based on camera trajectory, focusing on predictive loading rather than static MPI chunks [G].
+### ShardedVolume
+Uses dynamic sharding and potentially more complex data structures for distributed storage. Better for very large, dynamic datasets. [Link](12593-shardedvolume.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(S/P)$ for reading and processing, where $S$ is the number of slices and $P$ is the number of processors, assuming uniform workload [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(S/P)$ per process, as each process only loads its assigned slices [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales well for uniform-resolution data where I/O and computation are balanced across processors [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, efficient I/O for contiguous data, no need for complex data structures [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Load imbalance for adaptive-resolution data, boundary artifacts if not handled, overhead for sewing meshes [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Large uniform-resolution volumes on MPI clusters where efficient I/O and computation distribution are required [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Adaptive-resolution data without workload rebalancing [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(S/P)$ for reading and rendering, where $S$ is the number of slices and $P$ is the number of processors. Sewing is $O(V + T)$ where $V$ is total vertices and $T$ is total triangles [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(S_{chunk} \times \text{slice\_size})$ per process. Each process only loads its assigned chunk [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Linear scaling is expected if I/O and computation are balanced. Efficiency depends on the uniformity of the data and the overhead of sewing/compositing [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; efficient I/O due to contiguous slice access; no need for complex load balancing for uniform data [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Not suitable for adaptive-resolution data without modification; requires post-processing (sewing/compositing) to combine results; boundary artifacts if overlap is not handled [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Uniform-resolution volumes where I/O efficiency is critical and the number of processors is known and static [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Adaptive-resolution data; scenes with highly non-uniform computational cost per slice [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is described in the context of parallel volume visualization for time-varying datasets, specifically handling spatial division of a single time step [S2, unofficial]. It is based on the principle of spatial division of uniform resolution data, where the volume is partitioned along one axis to allow independent rendering by multiple processors [S2, unofficial]. The sewing step for isosurface extraction was introduced to handle the concatenation of partial meshes generated by parallel processes [S1].
+The method was described in the context of parallel isosurface extraction on GPU clusters [S1] and volume visualization of time-varying data [S2, unofficial]. It is a standard approach for data-parallel volume rendering where the data is partitioned spatially along one axis.
 
